# Locale, units and time

UK English is the authored documentation style, not a command to replace a household’s Home Assistant language, units or time preferences. Preserve dynamic values and their semantics while formatting them consistently with the native configuration.

## Scope and native ownership

Home Assistant profile settings supply localization preferences. Maintained civica Energy tables already read native number, clock and time-zone preferences; this broader contract covers other surfaces without claiming that they all have a dedicated adapter. [Official Home Assistant source](https://www.home-assistant.io/docs/configuration/user-configuration/), reviewed 11 November 2026.

This page completes the design specification for **HA-GAP-023**. Its examples are synthetic documentation, not a claim that a new native feature or adapter has been installed. The application integration must meet the task-specific acceptance below.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Required presentation | Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Measured value | Keep quantity and unit together using native formatting. | Do not convert a rate into a total or increase precision. |
| Date-only item | Keep the calendar date without inventing a midnight instant. | Do not shift an all-day task across dates by converting zones. |
| Timed observation | Label its observation/period meaning and relevant zone. | last_changed alone is not a new measurement timestamp. |
| Repeated local time | Show the offset or other unambiguous context. | 01:30 alone may identify two different instants. |
| Unknown value / translation | Use explicit missing text and preserve user-supplied names. | Do not substitute zero or silently translate identifiers. |

## Integration contract

1. Use native locale, number separators, unit and 12/24-hour preferences. Do not derive them from the selected civica palette or the browser language alone.
2. Keep date-only values, instants, durations and recurring local schedules as distinct data types. Convert only when the source contract calls for conversion.
3. At a daylight-saving transition, distinguish equal wall-clock labels by offset or full period context. Keep comparison-series source dates rather than relabelling them as the current day.
4. Use bidirectional isolation for identifiers inside right-to-left prose. Mirror layout where appropriate, but do not reverse physical directions, media content, units or chart meaning automatically.

## Accessible interaction

- Long translations and enlarged text must wrap without clipping names, units, field errors or primary actions.
- Make the spoken value include its unit and relevant time context. Abbreviations should not be the only explanation of an unfamiliar quantity.
- Avoid ambiguous numeric dates in authored examples. Mark an illustrative translation as unreviewed unless a suitable language review exists.

## Failure and recovery

- If a locale formatter fails, retain the source value and unit with an explicit fallback rather than producing NaN or an empty successful value.
- If the user changes a preference, reformat displayed values without changing stored measurements, schedule instants or identifiers.
- If the timezone is unknown, state that limitation instead of assuming Europe/London. Examples with a declared zone do not prescribe the live household zone.

## Complete examples

The examples use invented records. Forms validate and show local feedback only; they do not call Home Assistant, save preferences or claim a real device result. In-page links lead to the explanation shown in the same specimen.

